Measurements of joint polarization states for $W$ and $Z$ bosons in $W^{\pm}Z$ production processes have been reported. These measurements were derived from $139$ fb$^{-1}$ worth of data from proton-proton collisions at a $13$ TeV center-of-mass energy, obtained with the ATLAS detector at CERN's Large Hadron Collider.  $W^{\pm}Z$ candidate events, resulting from the decay paths $pp	o W^{\pm}Z 	o \ell^{\pm} 
u \ell' \ell'$ and $pp	o W^{\pm}Z 	o \ell^{\pm} 
u \ell' 
u$, were analyzed using the polarizations of the charged leptons from their respective decay paths.  These measurements are compared to theoretical predictions from the Standard Model and are found to be in line with the latter, at both leading and next-to-leading order.  These measurements, further subdivided by kinematic variables $p_T$ and rapidity of the W boson, do not exhibit notable discrepancies with predictions.  Expectations are that future data, particularly from high-luminosity runs, will enable more stringent tests of the Standard Model and potential anomalies in $W$ polarization, including possible polarization variations based on its direction of flight.

This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation.
